1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,525
This video is about grammars. A grammar is a finite way to write down a language.

2
00:00:05,975 --> 00:00:20,354
We learn the parts of a grammar, how a derivation works, and how to draw a parse tree. We also learn the classes of grammars, from regular to unrestricted. At the end we solve three question bank questions.

3
00:00:20,804 --> 00:00:26,479
The compiler front end has two stages. The lexer cuts the characters into tokens.

4
00:00:26,929 --> 00:00:30,449
The parser groups the tokens into a tree, the A S T.

5
00:00:30,899 --> 00:00:37,491
For the lexer, the specification is a set of regular expressions. The machine is a D F A.

6
00:00:37,941 --> 00:00:42,613
For the parser, the specification is a grammar. The machine is a parser.

7
00:00:43,063 --> 00:00:53,580
Regular expressions cannot describe nested structure, such as these nested parentheses. A grammar can. This chapter of the notes is about grammars.

8
00:00:54,030 --> 00:01:02,371
Start with the basic idea. A language is a set of strings. Here the set has three strings: a, a b, and a b c.

9
00:01:02,821 --> 00:01:09,584
A recognizer is a program that checks if an input string is in the set. It answers yes or no.

10
00:01:10,034 --> 00:01:16,498
If the language is finite, we can write all its strings down. This example has only three.

11
00:01:16,948 --> 00:01:23,711
But almost all languages are infinite. We cannot list an infinite set in finite space and time.

12
00:01:24,161 --> 00:01:32,651
So we need a finite notation. The notes list four: grammars, regular expressions, set comprehensions, and words.

13
00:01:33,101 --> 00:01:45,048
Here are three languages from the notes, each in four notations. In words: language 1 is any number of x's. Language 2 is an x followed by some y's.

14
00:01:45,498 --> 00:01:48,869
The last column lists some strings of each language.

15
00:01:49,319 --> 00:01:53,308
As regular expressions: x star, and x y plus.

16
00:01:53,758 --> 00:02:01,139
As set comprehensions: x to the n for n at least 0, and x y to the n for n at least 1.

17
00:02:01,589 --> 00:02:12,640
As grammars. Language 1: S goes to x S, or epsilon. Language 2: S goes to x y T, and T goes to y T or epsilon.

18
00:02:13,090 --> 00:02:19,831
A grammar repeats with recursion: S appears on its own right side, and T on its own right side.

19
00:02:20,281 --> 00:02:28,964
Language 3 counts two things: n x's, then the same number of y's. A set comprehension and a grammar can describe it.

20
00:02:29,414 --> 00:02:39,142
A regular expression cannot, because a regex cannot count. Some languages are impossible to specify in some notations.

21
00:02:39,592 --> 00:02:49,021
Look at the grammar for language 2 in detail. It has two rules. S goes to x y T. T goes to y T, or epsilon.

22
00:02:49,471 --> 00:03:00,437
S and T are nonterminals. A nonterminal stands for a part of the language. It must be rewritten. In the notes, nonterminals start with a capital letter.

23
00:03:00,887 --> 00:03:07,649
x and y are terminals. Terminals are the tokens that come from the lexer. They are never rewritten.

24
00:03:08,099 --> 00:03:13,881
Each rule is a production. It has a left hand side, an arrow, and a right hand side.

25
00:03:14,331 --> 00:03:22,117
The bar separates alternatives for the same left hand side. T goes to y T, or T goes to epsilon.

26
00:03:22,567 --> 00:03:24,850
Epsilon is the empty string.

27
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:32,233
The start symbol is the left hand side of the first rule. Here it is S. Every derivation starts there.

28
00:03:32,683 --> 00:03:48,342
Now use a grammar. A derivation starts with the start symbol and rewrites one nonterminal per step, with one production. Derive x x x with the grammar S goes to x S, or epsilon. Line 1 is S.

29
00:03:48,792 --> 00:03:58,392
Rewrite S with S goes to x S. The new symbols are orange. On the right, the parse tree grows: S gets two children, x and S.

30
00:03:58,842 --> 00:04:04,410
Rewrite the new S, again with S goes to x S. Now we have x x S.

31
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:06,972
Once more. x x x S.

32
00:04:07,422 --> 00:04:12,755
Now choose the other alternative, S goes to epsilon. The S disappears.

33
00:04:13,205 --> 00:04:19,093
The string x x x has no nonterminals left. So x x x is in the language.

34
00:04:19,543 --> 00:04:27,586
The parse tree shows which rule made which part. Its leaves, read left to right, are x, x, x, and the empty string.

35
00:04:28,036 --> 00:04:33,860
Another one. Derive x y y with the grammar for language 2. Start with S.

36
00:04:34,310 --> 00:04:42,907
S has only one production. S goes to x y T. It makes two terminals, x and y, and one nonterminal, T.

37
00:04:43,357 --> 00:04:48,520
The input still has one more y. So rewrite T with T goes to y T.

38
00:04:48,970 --> 00:04:57,589
Now the input is complete. Rewrite T with epsilon. The result is x y y, so x y y is in the language.

39
00:04:58,039 --> 00:05:05,932
Language 3: S goes to x S y, or x y, or epsilon. Derive x x x y y y.

40
00:05:06,382 --> 00:05:12,313
S goes to x S y. One x on the left, one y on the right, and S in the middle.

41
00:05:12,763 --> 00:05:17,605
Again, S goes to x S y. Now there are two x's and two y's.

42
00:05:18,055 --> 00:05:22,855
Last, S goes to x y. The result is three x's and three y's.

43
00:05:23,305 --> 00:05:28,852
Each step adds one x and one y at the same time. That is how a grammar counts.

44
00:05:29,302 --> 00:05:33,227
The tree is nested: each S is inside the S above it.

45
00:05:33,677 --> 00:05:46,563
A second derivation also exists. Use x S y three times, then S goes to epsilon. Two derivations for one string is ambiguity. Video 18 covers it.

46
00:05:47,013 --> 00:06:09,562
Language 5 in the notes is nested subtractions. S goes to E. E goes to E minus T, or T. T goes to an expression in parentheses, or an integer. Build the tree for 9 minus, open parenthesis, 5 minus 2, close parenthesis. At the top, S goes to E, and E goes to E minus T.

47
00:06:10,012 --> 00:06:13,191
The left E goes to T, then int, then 9.

48
00:06:13,641 --> 00:06:17,161
The right T goes to parentheses with a new E inside.

49
00:06:17,611 --> 00:06:22,859
Inside the parentheses, the same rule repeats: E goes to E minus T.

50
00:06:23,309 --> 00:06:26,423
The inner left E goes to T, int, and 5.

51
00:06:26,873 --> 00:06:29,177
The inner T goes to int, and 2.

52
00:06:29,627 --> 00:06:37,606
An E inside a T inside an E. Nesting to any depth is one of the things a regular expression cannot do.

53
00:06:38,056 --> 00:06:53,267
Grammars are classified by complexity. This is the Chomsky hierarchy from the notes. The outside class is unrestricted grammars. To parse them, you need a Turing machine, and the time has no reasonable limit.

54
00:06:53,717 --> 00:06:59,242
Inside are the context-sensitive grammars. They need a linear bounded automaton.

55
00:06:59,692 --> 00:07:10,316
Inside those are the context-free grammars, C F Gs. A nondeterministic pushdown automaton parses them in order n cubed time.

56
00:07:10,766 --> 00:07:21,177
Inside the context-free class is L L one. You can write an L L one parser by hand with recursive descent. It runs in linear time.

57
00:07:21,627 --> 00:07:28,347
P E G, parsing expression grammars, overlap with L L one. They come later in the course.

58
00:07:28,797 --> 00:07:34,941
In the centre are the regular grammars. A finite automaton parses them in linear time.

59
00:07:35,391 --> 00:07:40,617
This course works mostly with regular, L L one and context-free grammars.

60
00:07:41,067 --> 00:07:52,310
When you design a language, always choose the simplest possible class. Simpler grammars take less time and less machinery to parse. The notes call this the kiss rule.

61
00:07:52,760 --> 00:08:03,405
Engineers do not always follow it. The full grammar for V H D L is at least ambiguous. a of i can be an array access or a function call.

62
00:08:03,855 --> 00:08:18,746
The language that the airline industry uses to encode ticket information uses an unrestricted grammar. It was designed before grammar complexity was well understood, and it still causes trouble for engineers.

63
00:08:19,196 --> 00:08:27,815
Two grammars can describe the same language. Here is a grammar for x star. It is right recursive: S goes to x S.

64
00:08:28,265 --> 00:08:34,153
Here is a second grammar for x star. It is left recursive: S goes to S x.

65
00:08:34,603 --> 00:08:41,003
Both grammars give the same set of strings. A python check compared all strings up to length 8.

66
00:08:41,453 --> 00:08:46,061
Two grammars that accept exactly the same strings are weakly equivalent.

67
00:08:46,511 --> 00:08:53,103
But the parse trees are different. For x x, the left grammar gives a tree that leans to the right.

68
00:08:53,553 --> 00:08:56,646
The right grammar gives a tree that leans to the left.

69
00:08:57,096 --> 00:09:05,501
Two grammars that give the same strings and the same parse trees are strongly equivalent. These two are only weakly equivalent.

70
00:09:05,951 --> 00:09:11,285
Engineers often refactor a complex grammar into an equivalent, simpler one.

71
00:09:11,735 --> 00:09:23,724
How do we test if a grammar is context free? Here is a grammar from the notes for a to the n, b to the n, c to the n. That is n a's, then n b's, then n c's.

72
00:09:24,174 --> 00:09:31,555
The test: a context-free grammar has exactly one nonterminal on the left hand side of every production.

73
00:09:32,005 --> 00:09:36,101
The top two rules pass. They rewrite S, wherever it appears.

74
00:09:36,551 --> 00:09:42,631
All the other rules have two symbols on the left. For example, C B goes to H B.

75
00:09:43,081 --> 00:09:53,236
The rule a B goes to a b can rewrite B only when B comes right after an a. The context decides, so the grammar is context sensitive.

76
00:09:53,686 --> 00:10:06,785
Multiple symbols on the left hand side mean the grammar is not context free. The canonical example is this language, which counts three things. No context-free grammar exists for it.

77
00:10:07,235 --> 00:10:17,688
Here is a full derivation of a a b b c c with this grammar. Start with S. Lowercase letters are terminals. Uppercase letters are nonterminals.

78
00:10:18,138 --> 00:10:20,037
S goes to a S B C.

79
00:10:20,487 --> 00:10:25,820
Then S goes to a B C. Now we have two a's, but the B's and C's are mixed.

80
00:10:26,270 --> 00:10:32,350
The C's must move to the right of the B's. The rule C B goes to H B starts a swap.

81
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:34,720
H B goes to H C.

82
00:10:35,170 --> 00:10:43,170
H C goes to B C. These three rules together swap C and B. H is a helper. Now the B's are together.

83
00:10:43,620 --> 00:10:48,079
a B goes to a b. A B becomes a b only after an a.

84
00:10:48,529 --> 00:10:50,427
b B goes to b b.

85
00:10:50,877 --> 00:10:52,733
b C goes to b c.

86
00:10:53,183 --> 00:10:57,514
c C goes to c c. The result is a a b b c c.

87
00:10:57,964 --> 00:11:07,116
Our python search found a flaw in this figure. It also derives a a a b b c c c c, because one H can help in two swaps.

88
00:11:07,566 --> 00:11:16,995
The source that the notes cite uses two helpers, Z and W. That version derives only a to the n, b to the n, c to the n.

89
00:11:17,445 --> 00:11:23,376
For the exam, the important point is the test: several symbols on a left hand side.

90
00:11:23,826 --> 00:11:32,807
Is a grammar regular? First, the three common cases that are outside the regular class. Engineers need all three in practice.

91
00:11:33,257 --> 00:11:40,340
One. Unlimited balanced parentheses. n open parentheses, an a, then n close parentheses.

92
00:11:40,790 --> 00:11:48,875
Two. Expression nesting. An expression can contain an expression in parentheses, or two expressions with a plus.

93
00:11:49,325 --> 00:11:56,003
Three. Indefinite counting. n a's followed by n b's. S goes to a S b, or a b.

94
00:11:56,453 --> 00:12:12,154
These are the three main features to look for. Each one needs a count with no limit. A finite automaton has a finite number of states, so it cannot keep such a count. If the language needs one of them, the grammar is not regular.

95
00:12:12,604 --> 00:12:21,201
Now the test to prove that a grammar is regular. The left hand side must be one nonterminal, as in a context-free grammar.

96
00:12:21,651 --> 00:12:26,089
The right hand side must be the empty string, or a single terminal.

97
00:12:26,539 --> 00:12:33,067
Or it must be linear. Right linear is a terminal followed by a nonterminal, such as x S.

98
00:12:33,517 --> 00:12:38,530
Left linear is a nonterminal followed by a terminal, such as S x.

99
00:12:38,980 --> 00:12:47,364
All the rules must be right linear, or all must be left linear. If the two forms are mixed, the grammar might not be regular.

100
00:12:47,814 --> 00:12:57,286
Test the grammar for language 2. The rule S goes to x y T has two terminals before the nonterminal. That is not one of the forms.

101
00:12:57,736 --> 00:13:13,651
Refactor it. Add a nonterminal U. S goes to x U, and U goes to y T. Now every rule is right linear, so the grammar is regular. A python check showed that both grammars give the same strings up to length 8.

102
00:13:14,101 --> 00:13:27,882
Language 3 has S goes to x S y. The nonterminal is in the middle, with terminals on both sides. That is not linear. The language counts two things, so no regular grammar exists for it.

103
00:13:28,332 --> 00:13:36,993
Here is our own example of why the two forms must not be mixed. S goes to a T, or epsilon. T goes to S b.

104
00:13:37,443 --> 00:13:42,713
The rule S goes to a T is right linear: a terminal, then a nonterminal.

105
00:13:43,163 --> 00:13:48,261
The rule T goes to S b is left linear: a nonterminal, then a terminal.

106
00:13:48,711 --> 00:13:52,615
Derive a a b b. Start with S. S goes to a T.

107
00:13:53,065 --> 00:13:57,311
T goes to S b. Now S is in the middle, between a and b.

108
00:13:57,761 --> 00:13:59,467
S goes to a T again.

109
00:13:59,917 --> 00:14:03,523
T goes to S b again. Now we have a a S b b.

110
00:14:03,973 --> 00:14:07,599
S goes to epsilon. The result is a a b b.

111
00:14:08,049 --> 00:14:17,713
This grammar gives n a's then n b's. A python check confirmed it up to length 8. So every rule is linear, but the mix is not regular.

112
00:14:18,163 --> 00:14:32,670
Now the question bank. Question 1.1.1. Engineers work in both measurement systems. Using this grammar, derive metric 3.875, and imperial 3 7 slash 8.

113
00:14:33,120 --> 00:14:37,451
Digits is one or more digits. It is a token from the lexer.

114
00:14:37,901 --> 00:14:39,543
Pause the video and try it.

115
00:14:39,993 --> 00:14:46,713
The input has four tokens: metric, 3, dot, 875. Start with Measurement.

116
00:14:47,163 --> 00:14:54,203
The first token is metric. So use the first alternative: Measurement goes to metric DecimalNumber.

117
00:14:54,653 --> 00:14:58,408
DecimalNumber has one production: Digits dot Digits.

118
00:14:58,858 --> 00:15:05,941
Now there are two nonterminals. We rewrite the leftmost one first. This is a leftmost derivation.

119
00:15:06,391 --> 00:15:10,977
The L L in L L one means left to right, leftmost derivation.

120
00:15:11,427 --> 00:15:14,350
The leftmost Digits matches the token 3.

121
00:15:14,800 --> 00:15:24,464
The last Digits matches 875. No nonterminals are left, and the string matches the input. The derivation is complete.

122
00:15:24,914 --> 00:15:31,421
Now imperial 3 7 slash 8. The input has five tokens. Start with Measurement.

123
00:15:31,871 --> 00:15:37,225
The first token is imperial. So Measurement goes to imperial FractionalNumber.

124
00:15:37,675 --> 00:15:45,462
FractionalNumber goes to Digits Digits slash Digits. The space between 3 and 7 separates two tokens.

125
00:15:45,912 --> 00:15:48,493
Rewrite the leftmost Digits: 3.

126
00:15:48,943 --> 00:15:51,013
Then the next Digits: 7.

127
00:15:51,463 --> 00:15:55,281
Then the last Digits: 8. The result matches the input.

128
00:15:55,731 --> 00:16:01,470
The posted solution does the three Digits steps in one line. That is fine on an exam.

129
00:16:01,920 --> 00:16:07,424
Question 1.1.2. Try to derive five sentences with this grammar.

130
00:16:07,874 --> 00:16:17,303
The parentheses group two alternatives. For example, Technical is Engineer calculates, or Architect draws, followed by design.

131
00:16:17,753 --> 00:16:19,396
Pause the video and try it.

132
00:16:19,846 --> 00:16:28,230
Engineer calculates design. ProfessionalSkills goes to Technical. Technical goes to Engineer calculates design.

133
00:16:28,680 --> 00:16:35,421
Physician examines patient. ProfessionalSkills goes to Medical, then Physician examines patient.

134
00:16:35,871 --> 00:16:41,845
Judge decides case. ProfessionalSkills goes to Legal, then Judge decides case.

135
00:16:42,295 --> 00:16:47,180
Each accepted sentence takes two steps. The first word decides the group.

136
00:16:47,630 --> 00:16:55,609
Engineer cuts patient. The word Engineer appears only in Technical. So ProfessionalSkills must go to Technical.

137
00:16:56,059 --> 00:17:01,925
Technical goes to Engineer calculates design. Engineer matches the first token.

138
00:17:02,375 --> 00:17:07,879
The grammar needs calculates next. The input has cuts. The derivation is stuck.

139
00:17:08,329 --> 00:17:16,009
Lawyer advocates design. The word Lawyer appears only in Legal. So ProfessionalSkills must go to Legal.

140
00:17:16,459 --> 00:17:21,259
Legal goes to Lawyer advocates case. Lawyer and advocates match.

141
00:17:21,709 --> 00:17:25,763
Then Legal needs case, but the input has design. Stuck again.

142
00:17:26,213 --> 00:17:34,362
When no derivation reaches the input, the string is not in the language. The posted solution writes: stuck, reject.

143
00:17:34,812 --> 00:17:39,228
Surgeon cuts patient, and Lawyer advocates case, would be accepted.

144
00:17:39,678 --> 00:17:50,387
Question 1.2.1. A simple calculator language with operator precedence and nested expressions. Use square brackets to show precedence.

145
00:17:50,837 --> 00:18:01,355
Derive five strings: 1 plus 2, 2 times 3, 1 plus 2 times 3, open 1 plus 2 close times 3, and 1 times 2 plus 3.

146
00:18:01,805 --> 00:18:03,447
Pause the video and try it.

147
00:18:03,897 --> 00:18:09,615
First see how the grammar encodes precedence. Expr is a sum of one or more Terms.

148
00:18:10,065 --> 00:18:13,073
Term is a product of one or more Factors.

149
00:18:13,523 --> 00:18:21,288
Factor is an integer, or a whole Expr in parentheses. Each level of precedence is its own nonterminal.

150
00:18:21,738 --> 00:18:29,930
Times is one level deeper than plus. So in every parse tree, a times sits below the plus nodes, and is done first.

151
00:18:30,380 --> 00:18:33,516
Start with 1 plus 2. S goes to Expr.

152
00:18:33,966 --> 00:18:38,083
The input has a plus. So Expr goes to Term plus Expr.

153
00:18:38,533 --> 00:18:45,723
The left Term must derive 1. Term goes to Factor, Factor goes to Int, and Int is the token 1.

154
00:18:46,173 --> 00:18:50,333
The right Expr has no more plus. So Expr goes to Term.

155
00:18:50,783 --> 00:18:58,527
Term goes to Factor, Factor goes to Int, and Int is the token 2. The derivation reaches 1 plus 2.

156
00:18:58,977 --> 00:19:03,094
In the tree, the plus is at the top, with 1 and 2 below it.

157
00:19:03,544 --> 00:19:06,168
2 times 3. S goes to Expr.

158
00:19:06,618 --> 00:19:10,501
There is no plus in the input. So Expr goes to Term.

159
00:19:10,951 --> 00:19:14,727
There is a times. So Term goes to Factor times Term.

160
00:19:15,177 --> 00:19:18,142
The left Factor goes to Int, and Int is 2.

161
00:19:18,592 --> 00:19:25,077
The right Term goes to Factor, then Int, then 3. The derivation reaches 2 times 3.

162
00:19:25,527 --> 00:19:28,834
1 plus 2 times 3. S goes to Expr.

163
00:19:29,284 --> 00:19:33,700
There is a plus in the input. So Expr goes to Term plus Expr.

164
00:19:34,150 --> 00:19:39,974
The left Term has no times before the plus. It goes to Factor, then Int, then 1.

165
00:19:40,424 --> 00:19:45,928
The right Expr must derive 2 times 3. It has no plus, so it goes to Term.

166
00:19:46,378 --> 00:19:48,767
That Term goes to Factor times Term.

167
00:19:49,217 --> 00:19:51,372
The Factor goes to Int, then 2.

168
00:19:51,822 --> 00:19:55,363
The last Term goes to Factor, then Int, then 3.

169
00:19:55,813 --> 00:20:00,101
With brackets, the result is 1 plus, bracket, 2 times 3.

170
00:20:00,551 --> 00:20:11,282
In the tree, the times is inside the right Expr, below the plus. So the multiplication is done first: 2 times 3 is 6, and 1 plus 6 is 7.

171
00:20:11,732 --> 00:20:15,167
1 times 2 plus 3. S goes to Expr.

172
00:20:15,617 --> 00:20:19,265
There is a plus. So Expr goes to Term plus Expr.

173
00:20:19,715 --> 00:20:24,856
The left Term must derive 1 times 2. Term goes to Factor times Term.

174
00:20:25,306 --> 00:20:27,247
Factor goes to Int, then 1.

175
00:20:27,697 --> 00:20:31,772
The inner Term must stop before the plus. So it goes to Factor.

176
00:20:32,222 --> 00:20:36,553
Then Int, then 2. The left side is complete: 1 times 2.

177
00:20:37,003 --> 00:20:39,008
The right Expr goes to Term.

178
00:20:39,458 --> 00:20:42,125
Term goes to Factor, Int, then 3.

179
00:20:42,575 --> 00:20:52,345
With brackets: bracket 1 times 2, plus 3. The times is again below the plus. 1 times 2 is 2, and 2 plus 3 is 5.

180
00:20:52,795 --> 00:20:56,955
Open 1 plus 2 close, times 3. S goes to Expr.

181
00:20:57,405 --> 00:21:04,232
The plus is inside the parentheses. Outside them, there is no plus. So Expr goes to Term.

182
00:21:04,682 --> 00:21:08,458
There is a times. So Term goes to Factor times Term.

183
00:21:08,908 --> 00:21:14,945
This Factor must derive the part in parentheses. Factor goes to open, Expr, close.

184
00:21:15,395 --> 00:21:21,326
Inside the parentheses, the rules repeat. The inner Expr goes to Term plus Expr.

185
00:21:21,776 --> 00:21:24,869
The inner Term goes to Factor, Int, then 1.

186
00:21:25,319 --> 00:21:27,367
The inner Expr goes to Term.

187
00:21:27,817 --> 00:21:29,716
Then Factor, Int, and 2.

188
00:21:30,166 --> 00:21:34,433
The last Term after the times goes to Factor, Int, then 3.

189
00:21:34,883 --> 00:21:45,123
Now the plus is below the times, because the parentheses put a whole Expr inside a Factor. 1 plus 2 is 3, and 3 times 3 is 9.

190
00:21:45,573 --> 00:21:54,533
Compare with the posted solution. In 2 times 3 and in 1 times 2 plus 3, it writes Term goes to Factor times Expr.

191
00:21:54,983 --> 00:22:05,628
The grammar has no such production. It says Term goes to Factor times Term. Our derivations use the grammar exactly, and a python check found each one.

192
00:22:06,078 --> 00:22:14,782
Some posted lines also do two rewrites at once. For example, Term plus Expr becomes Factor plus Term in one line.

193
00:22:15,232 --> 00:22:25,749
The posted solution says that on an exam you may skip the steps that only move down the grammar to an Int. But every step you write must use a real production.

194
00:22:26,199 --> 00:22:33,815
To summarize. A language is a set of strings. A grammar describes an infinite language in finite space.

195
00:22:34,265 --> 00:22:43,503
Nonterminals are rewritten. Terminals are the tokens. A derivation starts at the start symbol and uses one production per step.

196
00:22:43,953 --> 00:22:52,081
The parse tree records each rewrite. In a leftmost derivation, you always rewrite the leftmost nonterminal first.

197
00:22:52,531 --> 00:23:01,555
The classes nest: regular, L L one, context free, context sensitive, unrestricted. Always choose the simplest class.

198
00:23:02,005 --> 00:23:20,223
A context-free grammar has one nonterminal on every left hand side. A regular grammar is all right linear or all left linear. Counting, nesting and balanced parentheses are not regular. Next video: recursive descent, which turns a grammar into code.
